 "literal": "In the backwater blue lilies plucking, of the sea / white-headed waves playing in also was good. / Without separation, the playmates, appropriate things to perform, / in this place staying also she did not agree; to that place, / where gravel is laid waste, she went, alas! / Moving rain-clouds crawl on the peaks / of the sky-high, rising, lying-across mountain land.",
 "translation": "Plucking blue lilies in the backwaters and playing in the white-crested waves of the sea was indeed delightful. Yet, refusing to stay here and engage in familiar games with her inseparable playmates, she has gone, alas, to that place where her path lies over barren, gravelly wastes, heading toward his land of high mountains that rise to the sky, where moving rain-clouds crawl over the peaks.",
 "modern": "கழிகளில் நீலக் குவளை மலர்களைப் பறிப்பதும், கடலின் வெள்ளைத் தலை அலைகளில் விளையாடுவதும் மிகவும் நல்லது. தன்னைப் பிரியாத தோழியர் கூட்டத்துடன் சேர்ந்து விளையாட வேண்டிய விளையாட்டுகளை விளையாடி இங்கேயே தங்குவதற்கும் அவள் உடன்படவில்லை. வானளாவிய, மேகங்கள் தவழும் சிகரங்களைக் கொண்ட மலைநாட்டிற்குச் செல்வதற்காக, கற்கள் நிறைந்த பாழ்வழியில் அவள் அவனுடன் சென்றுவிட்டாளே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the foster-mother after the heroine has eloped with her lover. She fondly recalls how the heroine used to spend her days happily on the cool seashore, plucking blue lilies in the backwaters and playing in the white-crested waves. She laments that the girl refused to stay in this comfortable home with her inseparable playmates. Instead, she has chosen to walk through a harsh, desolate path covered in rough gravel to reach the hero's distant mountain country. The contrast between the cool, familiar coast and the hot, painful wasteland highlights the foster-mother's deep grief and anxiety for the young girl's journey.",
